---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import HalfLife from '@/components/tools/calc2/HalfLife.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="half-life-calculator">
  <HalfLife client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the half-life calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      In each half-life, half of what is left decays. The half-life calculator uses the decay law to find
      any one of four values from the other three: the amount left, the starting amount, the time passed
      or the half-life.
    </p>
    <pre><code>N  = N₀ × (½)^(t ÷ T½)
N₀ = N ÷ (½)^(t ÷ T½)
t  = T½ × log₂(N₀ ÷ N)
T½ = t ÷ log₂(N₀ ÷ N)
λ  = ln 2 ÷ T½            decay constant
τ  = 1 ÷ λ = T½ ÷ ln 2    mean life</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example: carbon dating</h2>
    <p>
      Carbon-14 has a half-life of 5,730 years. After 11,460 years, which is two half-lives, a sample keeps
      100 × 0.5² = 25% of its carbon-14. A bone with 30% left is 5,730 × log₂(100 ÷ 30) = 5,730 × 1.737 =
      9,953 years old.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Isotope</th><th>Half-life</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody class="tabular-nums">
        <tr><td>Carbon-14</td><td>5,730 years</td><td>Dating organic material</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Iodine-131</td><td>8.02 days</td><td>Thyroid treatment</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Radon-222</td><td>3.82 days</td><td>Indoor radon gas</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Uranium-238</td><td>4.468 billion years</td><td>Dating rocks</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The simulation</h2>
    <p>
      Each square is one atom. Each atom gets a random decay moment drawn with the probability the
      half-life gives, so after one half-life about half of the 400 squares are gone, but rarely exactly
      half. The ink curve is the formula; the grey dot under it is the count of the squares, which
      scatters around the curve. Long decays play sped up, and the label gives the scale, for example
      1 s shows 2,000 years.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The law holds for a large number of atoms. For a handful, the count jumps randomly around the curve.</li>
      <li>Carbon dating also needs a calibration curve, because the carbon-14 level in the air has changed over time.</li>
      <li>For a decay chain (an isotope that decays into another radioactive isotope), each step needs its own calculation.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
